  "sol": "<p>Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> have <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n \\ge 2\\)</span> vertices. Each degree is one of the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> numbers <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0, 1, \\dots, n - 1\\)</span>, since a vertex has at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n - 1\\)</span> possible neighbours. The values <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n - 1\\)</span> cannot both occur: a vertex of degree <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n - 1\\)</span> is adjacent to every other vertex, so no vertex has degree <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span>. So the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> degrees take at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n - 1\\)</span> different values, and by the pigeonhole principle two vertices have the same degree.</p>\n<p>A complete search confirms it for every graph on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2\\)</span> to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(6\\)</span> fixed vertices (a check of small cases; the proof above covers every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>):</p>\n<pre data-code=\"py\">def repeated_degree(n):\n    pairs = list(combinations(range(n), 2))\n    return all(len({len(nbhd(v, g)) for v in range(n)}) &lt; n for g in all_graphs(pairs))\n\nprint([(n, 2 ** (n * (n - 1) // 2), repeated_degree(n)) for n in range(2, 7)])</pre>\n<pre data-code=\"out\">[(2, 2, True), (3, 8, True), (4, 64, True), (5, 1024, True), (6, 32768, True)]</pre>"
