  "q": "<p>For which <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> is there a 3-regular graph on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> vertices? Prove that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> must be even and at least <span class=\"math inline\">\\(4\\)</span>, and give a construction for every even <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n \\ge 4\\)</span>.</p>",
  "hint": "<p>Use <span class=\"math inline\">\\(|E| = \\frac{3n}{2}\\)</span>. For the construction, put the vertices <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0, \\dots, n - 1\\)</span> on a circle, join neighbours on the circle, and join each vertex to the one opposite.</p>",
  "sol": "<p><em>Necessary.</em> By the handshake lemma <span class=\"math inline\">\\(3n = 2|E|\\)</span>, so <span class=\"math inline\">\\(3n\\)</span> is even, hence <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> is even. A vertex of degree <span class=\"math inline\">\\(3\\)</span> needs three other vertices, so <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n \\ge 4\\)</span>.</p>\n<p><em>Sufficient.</em> For even <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n \\ge 4\\)</span>, take vertices <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0, 1, \\dots, n - 1\\)</span> and join <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span> to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i + 1\\)</span> and to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i + n/2\\)</span> (numbers taken modulo <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>). Each vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span> then has the neighbours <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i - 1\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i + 1\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i + n/2\\)</span>; these are three different vertices because <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n \\ge 4\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n/2 \\ne \\pm 1 \\pmod n\\)</span>. So the graph is 3-regular. (For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n = 4\\)</span> this is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(K_4\\)</span>.)</p>\n<pre data-code=\"py\">def cubic(n):\n    return {frozenset((i, (i + 1) % n)) for i in range(n)} | {frozenset((i, (i + n // 2) % n)) for i in range(n)}\n\nprint([(n, sorted({len(nbhd(v, cubic(n))) for v in range(n)}), len(cubic(n))) for n in (4, 6, 8, 10)])</pre>\n<pre data-code=\"out\">[(4, [3], 6), (6, [3], 9), (8, [3], 12), (10, [3], 15)]</pre>"
